hat $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components increase as we move vertically away from the origin. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/state.json" "file_sha256" "063b2c62c2e929429d2be6666f0a83af25ad862030455b110c8d7becbbd11a3c"} {"question" "q-131192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "da2609dfb73f0162bc2599f430419dca3ba7eb7de4cd61195c39c1bb543815bd"} {"question" "q-131192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/state.json" "file_sha256" "063b2c62c2e929429d2be6666f0a83af25ad862030455b110c8d7becbbd11a3c"} {"question" "q-131192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and IV only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and IV only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/state.json" "file_sha256" "063b2c62c2e929429d2be6666f0a83af25ad862030455b110c8d7becbbd11a3c"} {"question" "q-131192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/state.json" "file_sha256" "063b2c62c2e929429d2be6666f0a83af25ad862030455b110c8d7becbbd11a3c"} {"question" "e-8740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=\\ln(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf{i}+ x^2e^{3y}\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ \nEvaluate $\\nabla \\cdot\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/example-8740.json" "file_sha256" "ba17b5ca14d1ff9f0c59ae107ce51b7152198d07f73c31570740c05ff6fa5a46"} {"question" "e-8740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+y))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{3y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2x+y}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf {F}$ at the point $(1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0) & =\\frac{2}{2(1)+0}+3(1)^{2}{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =1+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/example-8740.json" "file_sha256" "ba17b5ca14d1ff9f0c59ae107ce51b7152198d07f73c31570740c05ff6fa5a46"} {"question" "e-9489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(2x-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i}+(3y-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{j}+(x-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,2,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/example-9489.json" "file_sha256" "c556f7d41df37b812294f1a038adc4d1c13b08d64709cfe6a87c2d1931fadafa"} {"question" "e-9489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j} + R\\,\\mathbf k$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}+\\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((2x-y)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((3y-2z)^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}((x-2z)^{2}) \\\\  & =4(2x-y)+6(3y-2z)-4(x-2z) \\\\  & =4x+14y-4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf { F}$ at the point $(1,2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F(1,2,1) & =4(1)+14(2)-4(1) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/example-9489.json" "file_sha256" "c556f7d41df37b812294f1a038adc4d1c13b08d64709cfe6a87c2d1931fadafa"} {"question" "e-8927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/d37f2a445986b2aae60603fed3bef1b480d8f049dfe17a0cf26325ac2eb167d8.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F?$\n\n- $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F <                    0$\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/example-8927.json" "file_sha256" "8f6cb3f033f51ce8018cd8e269cf0e83e93ea87d88986b3cc932511cb6940c77"} {"question" "e-8927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\textrm{div}\\, \\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F > 0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F <         0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $\\nabla \\cdot  \\mathbf F =0$ at $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/assets/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F,$ we say that $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $\\mathbf F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044029/example-8927.json" "file_sha256" "8f6cb3f033f51ce8018cd8e269cf0e83e93ea87d88986b3cc932511cb6940c77"}] "usage" {"e-8740" [] "q-41817" [] "e-8927" [] "e-9489" []} "basis" 1672 "retractions" [[17592186134293 :question/problem "Given that $F = 2xy^{2}i + 4x^{2}yj$, what is $divF$?"] [17592186133615 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=\\ln(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf{i}+ x^2e^{3y}\\,\\mathbf{j}$.\nEvaluate $\\nabla \\cdot\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0)$."] [17592186133615 :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nis a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot F &= \\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (2x + y)) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}e^{3y}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{2x + y} + 3x^{2}e^{3y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf {F}$ at the point $(1,0)$, and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∇ \\cdot F)(1, 0) &= (2)/(2(1) + 0) + 3(1)^{2}e^{3(0)} \\\\\n&= 1 + 3 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133628 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(2x-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i}+(3y-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{j}+(x-2z)^2\\,\\mathbf{k}$. Evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,2,1)$."] [17592186133628 :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j} + R\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nis a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{F}=\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}+\\dfrac{\\partial R}{\\partial z}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇ \\cdot F &= \\frac{∂P}{∂x} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂y} + \\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}((2x - y)^{2}) + \\frac{∂}{∂y}((3y - 2z)^{2}) + \\frac{∂}{∂z}((x - 2z)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 4(2x - y) + 6(3y - 2z) - 4(x - 2z) \\\\\n&= 4x + 14y - 4z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf {F}$ at the point $(1,2,1)$, and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndivF(1, 2, 1) &= 4(1) + 14(2) - 4(1) \\\\\n&= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133619 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2131/Source/Images/b5bfdb81e347992660d868824f19dfd3.png)\n\n1. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = 0$\n2. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F > 0$\n3. $\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F < 0$\n4. $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal"] [17592186133619 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\, \\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F\n$$\n\ndenotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf F$.\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P$:\n\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a source at $P$, then $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F > 0$ at $P$.\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like a sink at $P$, then $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F < 0$ at $P$.\n- If $\\mathbf F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P$, then $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F =0$ at $P$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2131/Source/Images/a3948ed745c39ad63bafd542288743db.png)\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0\n$$\n\neverywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf F$, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P$, the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $\\mathbf F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n- Statement IV is true. Since $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = 0$ everywhere, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186132201 17592186132202 17592186132203 17592186132204 17592186132205] "immutable_fields" [[17592186132200 :answer-field/correct] [17592186132200 :answer-field/id] [17592186132200 :answer-field/key] [17592186132200 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
